# Terraform Naming and Layout

All Terraform authoring and review **must** follow these conventions.
Canonical reference: https://www.terraform-best-practices.com/naming

These rules apply to **Terraform identifiers** (resource, data, variable, output, module, and local names). Cloud-provider constraints (S3 bucket names, DNS labels, and similar) belong in **argument values**, not in block names.

## 1. General conventions

- Use `_` (underscore), never `-` (dash), in Terraform identifiers.
- Prefer lowercase letters and numbers.
- File names are the exception: use `-` inside a path segment (see section 6).
- Local names follow the same identifier rules as variables.

## 2. Resources and data sources

- Do **not** repeat the resource type in the name (partially or fully):
  - ✅ `resource "aws_route_table" "public" {}`
  - ❌ `resource "aws_route_table" "public_route_table" {}`
  - ❌ `resource "aws_route_table" "public_aws_route_table" {}`
- Use `this` when no descriptive name exists, or when the module creates a single resource of that type.
- Always use **singular** nouns for names.
- Use `-` **inside argument values** that humans will see (for example an RDS DNS name).
- Put `count` / `for_each` first in the block, then a blank line.
- Prefer `for_each` over `count` for collections, keyed by a stable identity (name, id).
- For create/skip toggles, use `count` with a **boolean** (`var.enabled ? 1 : 0`). Do not feed a boolean to `for_each` — it expects a map or set. Prefer that boolean over `length(...)` or similar.
- Put `tags` last among real arguments (when the resource supports them). After that: `depends_on`, then `lifecycle`, each separated by a blank line.

```hcl
resource "aws_nat_gateway" "this" {
  count = var.enabled ? 1 : 0

  allocation_id = ...
  subnet_id     = ...

  tags = {
    Name = ...
  }

  depends_on = [aws_internet_gateway.this]

  lifecycle {
    create_before_destroy = true
  }
}
```

## 3. Variables

- In resource-wrapping modules, reuse upstream argument names (`name`, `description`, `default`) from the provider "Argument Reference".
- Use the **plural** form when the type is `list(...)` or `map(...)` (including object attributes such as `public_subnets`).
- Order keys in a `variable` block as: `description`, `type`, `nullable`, `default`, `validation`, `sensitive`.
- **Always** set `description`, even when it seems obvious. Match upstream wording when it exists.
- Use positive names; avoid double negatives (`encryption_enabled`, not `encryption_disabled`).
- Set `nullable = false` when `null` must not override the default. Omit it (or set `true`) when `null` is a valid value.
- Prefer `object(...)` when attributes belong together (see section 4). Use scalar/`list`/`map` types for standalone values.
- Use a tighter type such as `map(map(string))` when every element shares that type.
- Use `any` only to stop type-checking at a given depth, or when several types must be accepted.
- `{}` is an object; use `tomap(...)` when a map is required.

## 4. Variable modeling (no hard-coding, keep context)

This section is mandatory when creating or changing Terraform files. It wins over "use the simplest type" when they conflict: prefer **cohesive, contextual data**.

- Always drive configurable values from a `variable` (or from `locals` derived from variables). Do **not** hard-code strings such as CIDRs, names, AZs, instance sizes, feature flags, or counts in `resource` / `data` blocks.
- Model variables as **contextual objects with attributes**, not as isolated scalars. Example: one `variable "vpc"` that owns `public_subnets` / `private_subnets` as lists of objects (`name`, `cidr`, …) — never a separate variable per subnet or per CIDR.
- Provider enumerations and fixed protocol details may stay inline when they are inherent to the resource (`protocol = "tcp"`), not environment-specific.
- For a collection of the same kind (subnets, listeners), use a **list of objects**, each item carrying its own attributes, and iterate with `for_each`.

❌ Avoid disconnected scalars:

```hcl
variable "public_subnet_cidr_a"  { type = string }
variable "public_subnet_cidr_b"  { type = string }
variable "private_subnet_cidr_a" { type = string }
variable "vpc_cidr"              { type = string }
```

✅ Prefer a contextual object and typed collections:

```hcl
variable "vpc" {
  description = "VPC definition, including subnet collections with per-subnet attributes."
  type = object({
    name = string
    cidr = string
    public_subnets = list(object({
      name              = string
      cidr              = string
      availability_zone = string
    }))
    private_subnets = list(object({
      name              = string
      cidr              = string
      availability_zone = string
    }))
  })
  nullable = false
}

resource "aws_subnet" "public" {
  for_each = { for s in var.vpc.public_subnets : s.name => s }

  vpc_id            = aws_vpc.this.id
  cidr_block        = each.value.cidr
  availability_zone = each.value.availability_zone

  tags = {
    Name = each.value.name
  }
}
```

Concrete values live in `*.tfvars` (or in `default`), **never** inline on the resource.

## 5. Outputs

Make outputs obvious to module consumers.

- Name structure: `{name}_{type}_{attribute}`
  - `{name}` — resource or data source name (`private` from `aws_subnet.private`)
  - `{type}` — resource type **without** the provider prefix (`subnet`)
  - `{attribute}` — returned attribute (`id`, `arn`)
- If the resource is named `this`, omit `{name}` (`vpc_id`, not `this_vpc_id`).
- If the value interpolates several resources, keep `{name}` and `{type}` as generic as possible.
- If the value is a list, use a **plural** name (`public_subnet_ids`).
- **Always** set `description`.
- Set `sensitive = true` only when the value is a secret or credential. The flag taints every downstream reference — do not spread it.
- Use `try(...)` for optional or indexed resources instead of `element(concat(...))`.

## 6. File layout

Split resources **by component**. One resource (or a cohesive group with the same purpose) per file.

**Pattern:** `<domain>.<component>.tf`

- `<domain>` groups related resources (for example `vpc`).
- `<component>` is kebab-case and describes that file.
- The domain root file with no suffix (`vpc.tf`) holds the central resource of that domain.

Example:

```
vpc.tf                        # the VPC itself (aws_vpc)
vpc.public-subnets.tf         # public subnets
vpc.private-subnets.tf        # private subnets
vpc.public-route-table.tf     # public route table + associations
vpc.private-route-table.tf    # private route table + associations
vpc.internet-gateway.tf       # internet gateway
vpc.nat-gateway.tf            # NAT gateway + EIP
```

- Always use this pattern for new resources — do not dump everything into a single `main.tf`.
- Keep the `<domain>` prefix consistent across files in the same domain.
- In **file names**, use `-` inside a segment (`public-route-table`). That dash rule applies only to files; Terraform identifiers still use `_`.
- Keep `variables.tf`, `outputs.tf`, and `versions.tf` / `providers.tf` as the conventional module-root files.

## 7. Modules

- Name module blocks with `_`, never `-`: `module "public_subnets"`, not `module "public-subnets"`.
- Do not repeat the module source in the name (`module "vpc"`, not `module "vpc_vpc"`).
- Use `this` when the root module instantiates a single instance of that module with no better name.
